  </div>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════
       HOW TO USE
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div slot="how-to-use">
    <h2 style="font-family:'Sora',sans-serif; font-size:1.2rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.75rem;">How to use this tool</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary); font-size:0.9rem; line-height:1.75; margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
      Choose between two generation modes using the tabs at the top. <strong>Random Password</strong> generates a character-level password — adjust length and which character sets to include, then click Generate. <strong>Passphrase</strong> builds a memorable sequence of random dictionary words, ideal for master passwords or any credential you'll need to type by hand.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary); font-size:0.9rem; line-height:1.75; margin-bottom:0.75rem;">
      For passphrases, slide the word count up to increase entropy, pick a separator style, and optionally add capitalisation, a trailing number, or a symbol to satisfy site requirements. The entropy and crack-time meters update instantly so you can see the security trade-off as you adjust each option.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary); font-size:0.9rem; line-height:1.75;">
      Both modes display a SHA-256 hash of the generated credential — useful for developer testing and hash verification — and a one-click copy button.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════
       HOW IT WORKS
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div slot="how-it-works">
    <h2 style="font-family:'Sora',sans-serif; font-size:1.2rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:0.75rem;">How entropy and strength are calculated</h2>
    <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.75rem;">
      {[
        ['Cryptographic Generation', 'Both modes use window.crypto.getRandomValues(), which draws from the operating system\'s CSPRNG. For passwords, each character is chosen with rejection sampling to eliminate modulo bias. For passphrases, each word index is chosen from the 7,776-word EFF Diceware list using the same bias-free method.'],
        ['Password Entropy', 'Entropy (bits) = length × log₂(charset size). Each active character set adds to the charset: lowercase +26, uppercase +26, digits +10, symbols +32. Enabling all four sets gives a charset of ~94, yielding ~104 bits for a 16-character password.'],
        ['Passphrase Entropy (Diceware Model)', 'Each word is drawn from a 7,776-word list, so each word contributes log₂(7776) ≈ 12.92 bits. A 5-word passphrase yields ~64.6 bits. Appending a 2-digit number adds log₂(100) ≈ 6.64 bits; a symbol from a 32-character set adds log₂(32) = 5 bits. Capitalisation does not add measurable entropy because an attacker aware of the option can easily test it.'],
        ['Strength Thresholds', 'Very Weak: <28 bits. Weak: 28–35. Fair: 36–59. Strong: 60–127. Very Strong: 128+. For personal accounts, 60+ bits is adequate. For master passwords, key derivation, or long-lived credentials, target 80–128 bits. The NIST SP 800-63B guidance recommends a minimum of 8 characters but does not set an entropy floor — length and randomness matter more than character rules.'],
        ['Crack Time Estimate', 'Displayed as time to exhaust half the search space at 10⁹ guesses per second — a realistic estimate for an offline attack against a fast hash (SHA-256, MD5). For slow hashes (bcrypt, Argon2id), multiply by the work factor; a bcrypt cost-12 reduces effective speed to ~50,000/s, making even weaker passwords practically uncrackable offline.'],
        ['SHA-256 Hash', 'Computed client-side via SubtleCrypto.digest(). Use it to verify a credential\'s integrity or for developer testing. Never use SHA-256 alone for production password storage — use bcrypt, Argon2id, or scrypt with per-user salt and a calibrated work factor.'],
      ].map(([label, desc]) => (
        <div style="background:var(--bg-surface); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:0.625rem; padding:0.875rem 1rem;">
