  ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div slot="faq">
    <h2 style="font-family:'Sora',sans-serif; font-size:1.2rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.75rem;" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
      {[
        {
          q: 'Is a passphrase stronger than a random password?',
          a: 'It depends on length and configuration. A 5-word Diceware passphrase yields ~65 bits — roughly equivalent to a 10-character fully random password. A 7-word passphrase (~90 bits) exceeds what a 14-character mixed password provides, while being far easier to remember and type. For master passwords, passphrases are generally preferred precisely because their memorability means you\'re less likely to write them down.'
        },
        {
          q: 'How many bits of entropy does a strong password need?',
          a: 'For general personal accounts, 60+ bits is considered strong. For high-value accounts (financial, admin, email), target 80+ bits. A 16-character password using all four character sets yields approximately 104 bits — enough to resist even distributed brute-force attacks with current hardware.'
        },
        {
          q: 'Why is capitalisation not counted in passphrase entropy?',
          a: 'When a password policy or generator applies capitalisation deterministically (e.g. always capitalise first letter), an attacker who knows the rule only needs to test one variant per word combination. It adds no information to the attacker\'s search space. Entropy is a measure of the attacker\'s uncertainty, not the output\'s visual complexity.'
        },
        {
          q: 'Is this generator truly random?',
          a: 'Yes. Both modes use window.crypto.getRandomValues(), which draws from the operating system\'s cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generator (CSPRNG). This is fundamentally different from Math.random(), which is not cryptographically secure and must never be used for security-sensitive generation.'
        },
        {
          q: 'Should I use a password manager?',
          a: 'Absolutely. A password manager lets you use a unique, randomly generated password for every service without memorising them. This prevents credential stuffing attacks where a leaked password from one site is tested on others. Recommended options include Bitwarden (open source, free), 1Password, and KeePass (local, offline). Use a long passphrase as your manager\'s master password — it\'s the only one you need to remember.'
        },
        {
          q: 'Is SHA-256 safe for storing passwords?',
          a: 'No. SHA-256 is a fast general-purpose hash designed for data integrity — attackers can compute billions of SHA-256 hashes per second on consumer GPUs. Passwords must be stored using slow, purpose-built algorithms: bcrypt, Argon2id, or scrypt, all of which include salting and configurable work factors to resist hardware acceleration.'
        },
        {
          q: 'What is the EFF Diceware word list?',
          a: 'The EFF (Electronic Frontier Foundation) published a curated 7,776-word list in 2016 designed specifically for passphrase generation. Each word was selected to be memorable, unambiguous, and free of offensive terms. Using exactly 7,776 words (6⁵) allows each word to correspond to a five-dice roll, giving log₂(7776) ≈ 12.92 bits of entropy per word. This tool uses a JavaScript approximation of that list for fully in-browser generation.'
        },
      ].map(item => (
        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"
          style="background:var(--bg-surface); border:1.5px solid var(--border); border-radius:0.875rem; padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;">
